//BUG="a reference can be taken to a void variable, which has no storage to point at"
//ERR any

// The one place where erasure has no sensible answer. Once a void variable has
// no storage in C there is no address for `&r` to produce, so the compiler
// should reject this rather than emit a `void*` pointing at a variable it did
// not declare.
//
// Today it compiles, and the C fails on the erased variable rather than on the
// reference: `void var0;void* var1;`. Once void locals are dropped the second
// half of that line has nothing left to initialise from, so this needs a real
// diagnostic of its own.
//
// No //MSG, because the message does not exist yet; add one with the fix, and
// note that the span will land on `&r`.

fn nothing
    ret

fn main
    r = nothing()
    p = &r
    out "ok"
